  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/713A3C">Definition <span data-tag="713A3C">1.1.5.14</span>.</a>homotopy category!of a simplicial set Let $S$ be a simplicial set. The <em>homotopy category</em> $\rm {h} \it {S}$ is defined to be the homotopy category $\rm {h} \it { \sCoNerve [S]}$ of the simplicial category $\sCoNerve [S]$.hS@$\rm {h} \it {S}$ We will often view $\rm {h} \it {S}$ as a category enriched over the homotopy category $\calH $ of spaces via the construction of §<a href="/tag/8F9A99" data-tag="8F9A99">1.1.4</a>: that is, for every pair of vertices $x,y \in S$, we have $\bHom _{ \rm {h} \it {S}}(x,y) = [ \bHom _{ \sCoNerve [S]}(x,y) ]$. A map $f: S \rightarrow T$ of simplicial sets is a <em>categorical equivalence</em> if the induced map $\rm {h} \it {S} \rightarrow \rm {h} \it {T}$ is an equivalence of $\calH $-enriched categories.categorical equivalence </p>
